<main>
<section class="hero" id="home"><div class="wrap hero-grid"><div><div class="eyebrow">A closer look at seeing</div><h1>Your eyes see.<br>Your brain <span>interprets.</span></h1><p class="lead">The world feels direct and obvious. But vision is an active process—and sometimes your brain’s best guess gets it wrong.</p><div class="actions"><a class="btn primary" href="#illusions">Explore illusions <span aria-hidden="true">↗</span></a><a class="btn secondary" href="#quiz">Test your perception</a></div></div><div class="hero-art" aria-label="Stylized abstract eye illustration"><div class="eye" aria-hidden="true"><div class="iris"><div class="pupil"></div></div></div><div class="art-label">Same eyes. Different interpretations.</div></div></div></section>
<section class="section" id="guide"><div class="wrap"><div class="section-head"><div><div class="eyebrow">01 / The science</div><h2>A quick guide to <span style="color:var(--accent)">seeing</span></h2></div><p>Your brain doesn’t simply record an image. It combines incoming signals with context, experience, and expectations to create a useful interpretation.</p></div><div class="steps"><article class="step"><div class="num">1</div><h3>Light enters</h3><p>Light reflected from objects reaches the retina, where photoreceptors convert it into neural signals.</p></article><article class="step"><div class="num">2</div><h3>Signals are processed</h3><p>Visual pathways analyze features such as edges, contrast, color, depth, and motion.</p></article><article class="step"><div class="num">3</div><h3>Context adds meaning</h3><p>Your brain uses surrounding cues, prior experience, and expectations to interpret what those signals mean.</p></article><article class="step"><div class="num">4</div><h3>Perception emerges</h3><p>The result is a coherent experience—not a perfect, pixel-by-pixel copy of the outside world.</p></article></div><div class="science-note"><strong>Why illusions work:</strong> many illusions exploit normally useful visual processes. The effect depends on the image and viewing conditions; not every illusion works the same way for everyone.</div></div></section>
<section class="section" id="illusions"><div class="wrap"><div class="section-head"><div><div class="eyebrow">02 / Look twice</div><h2>Optical illusion <span style="color:var(--accent)">gallery</span></h2></div><p>Take a moment with each image. Notice your first impression, then read what it demonstrates.</p></div><div class="react-row"><span style="color:var(--muted);font-size:.9rem">A few classic tricks for your visual system.</span><button class="small-btn" id="pauseMotion" aria-pressed="false">Pause motion</button></div><div class="gallery">
<article class="illusion-card"><div class="illusion-art" aria-label="High-contrast spiral pattern" role="img"><div class="spiral" id="spiral"></div></div><div class="illusion-info"><h3>The spiral effect</h3><p>High-contrast patterns can create a strong impression of movement, even when the image is static.</p><span class="tag">Motion perception</span></div></article>
<article class="illusion-card"><div class="illusion-art face-vase" role="img" aria-label="Black background with a white vase-shaped silhouette"><div class="vase-shape"></div></div><div class="illusion-info"><h3>Face or vase?</h3><p>The same boundary can be interpreted as a central vase or as two faces in profile.</p><span class="tag">Figure and ground</span></div></article>
<article class="illusion-card"><div class="illusion-art" role="img" aria-label="Blue and yellow repeating dot pattern"><div class="dots"></div></div><div class="illusion-info"><h3>Pattern and motion</h3><p>Repeating shapes and contrasting colors can make still patterns feel lively or unstable.</p><span class="tag">Visual contrast</span></div></article>
<article class="illusion-card"><div class="illusion-art" role="img" aria-label="Stylized impossible staircase"><div class="stairs"></div></div><div class="illusion-info"><h3>Impossible geometry</h3><p>Local edges may look plausible even when the whole arrangement cannot exist as shown in ordinary 3D space.</p><span class="tag">Depth and perspective</span></div></article>
<article class="illusion-card"><div class="illusion-art"><div class="checker" role="img" aria-label="Checkerboard pattern with a shaded vertical cylinder"></div></div><div class="illusion-info"><h3>Checkerboard context</h3><p>A patch’s perceived brightness can change with its surroundings and the shadows we infer.</p><span class="tag">Brightness perception</span></div></article>
<article class="illusion-card"><div class="illusion-art muller" role="img" aria-label="Two horizontal lines with opposite arrowhead directions"><div class="linepair"></div><div class="linepair second"></div></div><div class="illusion-info"><h3>Müller-Lyer illusion</h3><p>Lines of equal length can look different when their arrowheads point in different directions.</p><span class="tag">Length and context</span></div></article>
</div></div></section>
<dialog class="explain-dialog" id="illusionDialog" aria-labelledby="dialogTitle"><div class="dialog-head"><h3 id="dialogTitle">Illusion explained</h3><button class="dialog-close" id="closeDialog" type="button" aria-label="Close explanation">Close ✕</button></div><div class="dialog-body"><div class="dialog-preview" id="dialogPreview" aria-hidden="true"></div><p id="dialogSummary"></p><h4>What your brain is doing</h4><p id="dialogMechanism"></p><div class="try-note"><strong>Try it:</strong> <span id="dialogTry"></span></div><div class="dialog-actions"><a class="btn secondary" id="videoSearch" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer" href="https://www.youtube.com/results?search_query=optical+illusions+explained">Watch related videos ↗</a><button class="btn primary" id="gotIt" type="button">Got it</button></div></div></dialog>
<section class="section" id="quiz"><div class="wrap quiz-wrap"><div class="quiz-intro"><div class="eyebrow">03 / Check your understanding</div><h2 style="font-size:clamp(2.2rem,4vw,3.4rem);letter-spacing:-.06em;line-height:1.05;margin:12px 0">A quick <span style="color:var(--accent)">brain check.</span></h2><p>Five short questions. Choose the best answer and get feedback as you go.</p><p style="font-size:.85rem">No timer, no pressure—curiosity is the point.</p></div><div class="quiz-box" id="quizBox" aria-live="polite"><div class="progress-top"><span id="questionCount">Question 1 of 5</span><span id="scoreLabel">Score: 0</span></div><div class="progress" aria-hidden="true"><span id="progressBar"></span></div><h3 id="question">What does an optical illusion reveal about vision?</h3><div class="answers" id="answers"></div><div class="feedback" id="feedback" role="status"></div><div class="quiz-controls"><button class="btn secondary" id="restart" hidden>Start over</button><button class="btn primary" id="next" disabled>Check answer</button></div></div></div></section>
<section class="section" style="padding:45px 0"><div class="wrap" style="display:flex;align-items:center;justify-content:space-between;gap:30px;flex-wrap:wrap"><div><div class="eyebrow">The big picture</div><h2 style="margin:10px 0;font-size:clamp(1.7rem,3vw,2.4rem);letter-spacing:-.05em">Perception is a process, <span style="color:var(--accent)">not a photograph.</span></h2><p style="color:var(--muted);max-width:690px;margin:0">Illusions are not evidence that your brain is broken. They help reveal the shortcuts and assumptions that usually make vision fast and useful.</p></div><a class="btn secondary" href="#home">Back to the top ↑</a></div></section>
