const illusionDetails = [
 {title:'The spiral effect',summary:'This high-contrast spiral pattern can seem to move, even though the printed pattern itself is still.',mechanism:'Visual motion systems respond to local contrasts and edges. In some patterns, tiny eye movements and how the visual system processes neighboring regions can contribute to a sensation of motion. The exact effect varies by pattern and viewer.',try:'Look at the center for a few seconds, then look at a blank wall. The impression may change. You can also pause the animation to compare.' ,search:'stationary spiral illusion explained visual perception'},
 {title:'Face or vase?',summary:'You may see a white vase in the center, or two dark face profiles looking toward one another.',mechanism:'Your visual system organizes an image into figure and background. Because the shared edge can belong to either interpretation, perception can switch between the vase and the faces.',try:'Try to alternate between seeing the vase and seeing both profiles. Notice that the same outline supports both interpretations.',search:'Rubin vase figure ground illusion explained'},
 {title:'Pattern and motion',summary:'A still arrangement of repeating colored shapes may appear to shimmer, drift, or feel visually unstable.',mechanism:'High contrast, repeated edges, color differences, and small involuntary eye movements can interact with motion-sensitive visual processing. This is a perceptual effect, not proof that the image is physically moving.',try:'Hold your gaze gently on one spot, then look away and back. Avoid staring if the pattern feels uncomfortable.',search:'peripheral drift illusion explained'},
 {title:'Impossible geometry',summary:'The staircase is drawn so that its individual edges look plausible, while the complete structure does not fit together as an ordinary three-dimensional object.',mechanism:'The brain uses perspective, shading, and familiar geometric cues to infer depth. An impossible figure makes these local cues conflict when you try to combine them into one consistent 3D scene.',try:'Trace the edges around the shape slowly and ask whether every step can connect into one continuous staircase in real space.',search:'impossible staircase optical illusion explained'},
 {title:'Checkerboard context',summary:'A checker pattern with a shaded cylinder demonstrates how the surroundings can influence how light or dark a surface appears.',mechanism:'The visual system estimates surface reflectance while accounting for illumination and shadows. Nearby contrast and assumptions about lighting can change perceived brightness, even when two patches have the same pixel value.',try:'Compare patches in different surroundings, then cover the surrounding pattern with your hand. Context can affect the judgment.',search:'checker shadow illusion Adelson explained'},
 {title:'Müller-Lyer illusion',summary:'Two lines with arrow-like ends can look like different lengths even when the central line segments are equal.',mechanism:'The angled fins alter the surrounding shape and context used in judging length. The precise explanation is still discussed, and the strength of the effect differs across people and settings.',try:'Estimate the lengths first, then measure the central horizontal segments with a ruler or compare them in an image editor.',search:'Muller Lyer illusion explained equal lines'}
];
